findings.body:
Removes the `ALLOWED` origin set and mirrors the request `Origin` (any value) into `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` while always setting `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`.

**Failure mode:** a page at `https://evil.example` sends a credentialed XHR/fetch to this API. The browser sees `ACAO: https://evil.example` + `ACAC: true`, includes the user’s cookies, and exposes the response body to the attacker script. That is full cross-site authenticated data read (and state-changing POSTs if the API accepts them).

Native mobile HTTP stacks do not enforce CORS, so they gain nothing from this. Mobile *webviews* that do enforce CORS still need an explicit allowlist — expand `ALLOWED`, do not reflect arbitrary origins.

Also, when `origin` is null this emits `ACAO: *` with `ACAC: true`, which browsers reject as an invalid CORS combination.

```suggestion
const ALLOWED = new Set([
  "https://app.example.com",
  // add known mobile-webview origins here
]);

export function corsHeaders(origin: string | null): Record<string, string> {
  if (origin && ALLOWED.has(origin)) {
    return {
      "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": origin,
      "Access-Control-Allow-Credentials": "true",
      Vary: "Origin",
    };
  }
  return {};
}
```
